-- title:
--   The function field of X₁(M) over ℚ̄ is essentially of finite type
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be a natural number, assumed non-zero. Write $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ and $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ for the fields of formal Laurent series `LaurentSeries ℚ` and `LaurentSeries (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)`, and let `x1FunctionField M` be the intermediate field `x1FunctionFieldC ℚ M` of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, the $q$-expansion function field attached to $X_1(M)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. Its base change `x1FunctionFieldBar M` is, by definition of `laurentBaseChange`, the intermediate field of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ generated by the image of `x1FunctionField M` under the coefficientwise embedding `coeffEmb (AlgebraicClosure ℚ)` of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$, i.e. $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\bigl(\mathbb{Q}(X_1(M))\bigr)$ inside $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$. The assertion is that this field, viewed as an algebra over the algebraic closure $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of $\mathbb{Q}$, satisfies `Algebra.EssFiniteType`: it is essentially of finite type over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, that is, a localisation of a finitely generated $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra.
--
--   This records that the $q$-expansion realisation of the function field of $X_1(M)$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is a function field in the sense required by the project's curve machinery, so that Riemann–Roch-type statements apply to it. It is used in the identification of the regular differentials of $X_1(M)_{\overline{\mathbb{Q}}}$ with weight-two cusp forms on $\Gamma_1(M)$, in the existence of a canonical divisor for `x1FunctionFieldBar`, and in the criterion for membership in its regular differentials.
